<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Ek' Balam</h1><p class="st-meta">700 CE – 850 CE (approx.) · capital Ek' Balam</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A walled Maya city in north-eastern Yucatán whose acropolis held the superb stucco tomb of its king Ukit Kan Le'k Tok'.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Ek' Balam, anciently Talol, was settled from the Preclassic period. It became the capital of a kingdom in the 8th century and reached its height after the ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukit-kan-lek-tok">Ukit Kan Le'k Tok'</a> arrived and took power in 770.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The city is enclosed by stone walls, with causeways leading out from gateways. Its great Acropolis contains the tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukit-kan-lek-tok">Ukit Kan Le'k Tok'</a>, who died around 801, with a doorway modelled as a monster's jaws and winged stucco figures. Painted capstones and murals carry many hieroglyphic texts, making Ek' Balam an important source on northern Maya kingship.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Royal activity at Ek' Balam faded after about 850, and the region came under the influence of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chichen-itza">Chichen Itza</a>. The stucco facade was uncovered in the late 1990s.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ek-balam.jpg" alt="Ek' Balam" width="320" height="208"><figcaption>Image: Photo by Dorothy Beals and Sarah Martin Original uploader was Elijahmeeks at en.wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ekbalam-Wall-Round-Building.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>700 CE – 850 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ek' Balam</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ukit Kan Le'k Tok'</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Maya religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Classic Maya</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Talol, Ek Balam</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atl